{"name":"Mandalay","hex":"#b57b2e","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Mandalay","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A city in central Burma (Myanmar)","A region of Burma (Myanmar)"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Mandalay"}],"entries":[{"title":"Mandalay","blocks":[{"text":"Mandalay is the second-largest city in Myanmar, after Yangon. It is located on the east bank of the Irrawaddy River, 631 km (392 mi) north of Yangon. In 2014, the city had a population of 1,225,553."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/Mandalay_-_The_worship_of_Buddha.jpg/330px-Mandalay_-_The_worship_of_Buddha.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mandalay","wikidata":"Q185518"},{"title":"Mandalay (poem)","blocks":[{"text":"\"Mandalay\" is a poem by Rudyard Kipling, written and published in 1890, and first collected in Barrack-Room Ballads, and Other Verses in 1892. The poem is set in colonial Burma, then part of British India. The protagonist is a Cockney working-class soldier, back in grey, restrictive London, recalling the time he felt free and had a Burmese girlfriend, now unattainably far away."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8c/Moulmeinfromgreatpagoda.JPG/330px-Moulmeinfromgreatpagoda.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mandalay_(poem)","wikidata":"Q1247778"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0c/Ku_Tho_Taw_Pagoda_%26_Sandar_Muni_Pagoda%2C_Mandalay.jpg/500px-Ku_Tho_Taw_Pagoda_%26_Sandar_Muni_Pagoda%2C_Mandalay.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0c/Ku_Tho_Taw_Pagoda_%26_Sandar_Muni_Pagoda%2C_Mandalay.jpg/1280px-Ku_Tho_Taw_Pagoda_%26_Sandar_Muni_Pagoda%2C_Mandalay.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.or